      "title": "Async Tasks and Polling",
      "concept": "The async keyword runs a long task in the background and poll controls how often Ansible checks on it, with poll: 0 meaning fire and forget. Async is how you run a task that takes longer than the connection timeout, such as a large download or a slow database migration.",
      "syntax": "- name: Long job\n  ansible.builtin.command: /opt/slow-job.sh\n  async: 600\n  poll: 10",
      "exampleCode": "- name: Long job in the background\n  ansible.builtin.command: echo working\n  async: 60\n  poll: 5",
      "exampleLanguage": "ansible",
      "mistake": "Setting async without poll and then wondering why the playbook moves on before the task has finished.",
      "bug": {
       "prompt": "Find the bug: this Ansible example should demonstrate Async Tasks and Polling, but it does not work correctly.",
       "buggyCode": "- name: Long job\n  ansible.builtin.command: /opt/slow-job.sh\n  async: 600",
       "fixedCode": "- name: Long job\n  ansible.builtin.command: /opt/slow-job.sh\n  async: 600\n  poll: 10",
       "explanation": "With poll: 0 Ansible fires the task and moves on; poll: 10 makes it check every ten seconds until the job finishes or the timeout is reached."
      },
