      "id": "terraform-modules-m2-l4",
      "slug": "terraform-modules-m2-l4",
      "title": "count and for_each in Modules",
      "concept": "The count and for_each meta-arguments create several instances of a resource or a module from one block, with for_each preferred because it keys each instance by a meaningful name instead of a number. Inside a module, for_each over a map creates one module instance per entry.",
      "syntax": "module \"bucket\" {\n  source   = \"./modules/bucket\"\n  for_each = toset([\"logs\", \"assets\"])\n  name     = each.key\n}",
      "exampleCode": "variable \"buckets\" {\n  type    = list(string)\n  default = [\"logs\", \"assets\"]\n}\n\noutput \"bucket_count\" {\n  value = length(var.buckets)\n}",
      "exampleLanguage": "terraform",
      "mistake": "Using count with a list and then removing the middle item, which renumbers every instance after it and recreates them.",
      "bug": {
       "prompt": "Find the bug: this Terraform example should demonstrate count and for_each in Modules, but it does not work correctly.",
       "buggyCode": "resource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"b\" {\n  count  = length(var.buckets)\n  bucket = var.buckets[count.index]\n}",
       "fixedCode": "resource \"aws_s3_bucket\" \"b\" {\n  for_each = toset(var.buckets)\n  bucket   = each.key\n}",
       "explanation": "count keys instances by position, so removing one shifts the rest and recreates them; for_each keys by name, so only the removed one is destroyed."
      },
