      "concept": "A read-only filesystem makes the container's filesystem immutable at run time, with --read-only, so an attacker who gets in cannot write a malicious file. Writable directories the application needs, such as /tmp, are mounted as tmpfs, which is memory and disappears with the container.",
      "syntax": "docker run --read-only --tmpfs /tmp myapp",
      "exampleCode": "FROM alpine:3.19\nRUN echo \"read-only filesystem\" > /readonly.txt\nCMD [\"cat\", \"/readonly.txt\"]",
      "exampleLanguage": "docker",
      "mistake": "Running a container read-only without a tmpfs for /tmp, so the application crashes the first time it writes a temporary file.",
