      "title": "Capabilities and Seccomp",
      "concept": "Linux capabilities are fine-grained privileges, and Docker drops most of them by default, so a container cannot do things like load kernel modules. The --cap-drop and --cap-add flags control them, and a seccomp profile restricts which system calls the container may make.",
      "syntax": "docker run --cap-drop=ALL --cap-add=NET_BIND_SERVICE myapp",
      "exampleCode": "FROM alpine:3.19\nRUN echo \"capabilities limited\" > /caps.txt\nCMD [\"cat\", \"/caps.txt\"]",
      "exampleLanguage": "docker",
      "mistake": "Running a container with --privileged to fix a permission problem, which gives it every capability and access to the host's devices.",
