      "title": "Rollbacks",
      "concept": "A rollback returns production to the previous release, which in Git means checking out the previous tag and deploying it. Rollbacks work because tags are permanent and the previous release is always available, which is why releases are tagged and never overwritten.",
      "syntax": "git checkout v1.2.0\ngit tag -l",
      "exampleCode": "git tag v1.2.0\ngit tag -l",
      "exampleLanguage": "git",
      "mistake": "Rolling back by reverting the commits on main, which rewrites the shared history instead of deploying the previous tag.",
